    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i'm particularly interested in the \"friction\" aspect though. in some of our simulations, we keep seeing these weird, unpredictable spikes in delay and minor disruptions that don't quite align with the standard flow models. how would you characterize the types of friction or interruptions that usually crop up in these systems?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Friction,\" in traffic terms, usually refers to those unpredictable delays that aren't accounted for by simple queuing models. In signalized intersections, this friction often manifests as driver hesitation at the stop line, or the \"platooning\" effect where a single slow vehicle gums up the entire queue. For roundabouts, the friction tends to be related to entry/exit conflicts, like drivers hesitating to yield or merging too aggressively, which can cause brief, sudden backups.\n\nThese unpredictable spikes are often caused by human behavior—the 'unpredictable' element—which is much harder to model than the predictable flow of a steady stream of traffic. Think of it like trying to predict the exact moment a group of very impatient, yet ultimately cautious, London commuters will decide to merge. They are less predictable than a perfectly timed traffic light sequence.",
      "source": "target",
